  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nHello,\r\n\r\nI would like to propose a new feature for the HTML specification: adding a \"volume\" attribute directly to the <audio> element. This would allow developers to set the initial volume level of audio files directly in HTML without relying on JavaScript.\r\n\r\nCurrently, to control the volume, developers need to use JavaScript like this:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nconst audio = document.querySelector('audio');\r\naudio.volume = 0.5;\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhile this works, it adds unnecessary complexity for a task that could be simplified by allowing a native HTML attribute. For example:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<audio controls src=\"audio.mp3\" volume=\"0.5\"></audio>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis feature would help streamline audio management, especially for simpler projects where using JavaScript adds overhead.\r\n\r\nI believe this enhancement would benefit a wide range of developers by making audio handling more intuitive and easier to implement. Thank you for considering this proposal.\r\n\r\nBest regards,",
